ing your run and is byte-identical in these sections — if it has landed when you refresh, cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers. Reuse the landed owners (canonicaljson closed shapes, scalar, sessrepo/sessstate/sessquery, sessckpt, matjournal, crashgate, fencing, sessprofile, provhost/resumesmoke, hosttrust/peeridentity/sshtransport, rpcwire/hostchannel, sessadapter, terminalbackend, localstore, secprim/secconftest) — never fork a second model; extend or adopt. Traceability: do NOT edit internal/traceability (ownership registry, digest pin, README measured-coverage subsection) in this first leaf — the Story's FINAL leaf carries the registry bindings and re-pin once the v0.7.0 registry is on trunk; document your clause coverage in a package-local TRACEABILITY.md and the conformance matrix instead. Evidence contract (repository DoD): every AC row driven through a production entry by a named committed test with a stated n-of-m ratio and call sites; negatives for every refusal; a narrowing mutant per gate executed by a shipped harness with per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits and an applied harmless SURVIVED control (PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__); crash/idempotency evidence for every durable write (landed no-replace + fsync + hooks + real-kill pattern) or an explicit purity bound; conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test or a stated bound; README package section with test commands and NO capability/CLI claim (no ax command exists); LOGBOOK entry newest-first. Managed Story worktree contract: .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-1cyj0q forked from current trunk; leave the candidate UNCOMMITTED; never commit on the Story branch, never edit .task-board directly, never self-accept; if origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-24z2b3` (LOGBOOK append conflicts are resolved with --replay-resolutions: copy the retained resolution-template.json header and add {\\\"checkpoint_oid\\\": <REBASE_HEAD original checkpoint>, \\\"path\\\": \\\"LOGBOOK.md\\\", \\\"sha256\\\": <hex of the full replacement>, \\\"content\\\": <base64>} keeping every entry newest-first), then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that appears in your git status and restore trunk content by hand (README/LOGBOOK purely additive; task-board.config.json == HEAD); before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first; all CI local; run the full configured 26-command validation suite once before handoff (command 21 is `cataloggen -adopted … -check`); hand off no later than 3.5 hours in — if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-24z2b3_results.md, TASK-260830-24z2b3_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-24z2b3_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-24z2b3 --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\\n=====REWORK=====\\nTHIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES EVERY EARLIER PRECONDITION ON TASK-260830-24z2b3, including TASK-260830-24z2b3_producer.md (still the scope of record for WHAT the leaf owns, but its execution instructions are replaced by this one). Do exactly what is written here.\\nRework run for TASK-260830-24z2b3 (implement-clone-bundle-and-canonical-session-types, first leaf of STORY-260830-1cyj0q). Change Request revision 2 was reviewed by the independent reviewer RUN-260917-c05bc8 (claude-opus-5 max) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: two P1 correctness holes, six P2, eight P3. Read TASK-260830-24z2b3_review-verdict-rev2.md end to end and TASK-260830-24z2b3_review-evidence-rev2.tar.gz (probes, refusal census, per-plant mutant logs with subprocess exits) before touching code. The reviewer verified a large part as clean and says so explicitly: all 16 closed shapes' member sets and all 6 vocabularies match the pinned text member-for-member, the row-21 exclusion gate, the provhost bound, the blob install path and the race gate are fine. Do NOT rewrite what holds, do NOT widen scope, and answer every finding explicitly. The reviewer's own rework scope is the spine of this brief.\\n1. P1-a — link descriptor identity to the entry. `VerifyDescriptorAgreement` checks the descriptor's self-claim and its blob_id/size against the entry, but nothing compares the entry's `blob_descriptor_id` with the identity of the descriptor bytes actually verified, so `BuildRawObjectManifest` seals a manifest whose entry names a descriptor that does not exist, and `VerifyRawManifestDescriptors` accepts any descriptor whose blob ID and size agree (probes P1/P1b/P1c; mutant `R-descriptor-id-unlinked2` survived 3/3). Compare the calculated descriptor identity with `blob_descriptor_id` at `buildRawEntries` AND at `VerifyRawManifestDescriptors`; add the negatives at both entries and a narrowing mutant for the new arm.\\n2. P1-b — enforce the AX number model on extension values. `omitSelfDigest`/`canonicalizeObject` in decode.go fork the identity path with lenient `encoding/json` + `canonicaljson.Canonicalize` — exactly what canonical.go:214-221 warns against — so `1.5`, `9007199254740993`, `1e2`, `-0` are admitted and `1<<60` is ROUNDED to 1152921504606847000 and the build continues, which SPEC.v0.6.0.md:305-310 forbids outright (\\\"Implementations MUST NOT round a value and continue\\\"). The same fork collapses nested duplicate members inside extension values, so a resealed `{\\\"a\\\":1,\\\"a\\\":2}` is admitted at Decode. Validate extension values recursively at Build and Decode (integer literals only, |n| <= 2^53-1, no fraction or exponent, nested objects duplicate-free at every depth) or route the omit-self digest through an owner entry that already carries the guarantee; add the negatives (1.5, exactly 2^53, 2^60, the nested duplicate) and a narrowing mutant that admits exactly 2^53.\\n3. P2-f + P2-a — adopt the landed environ gates instead of the local fork. `rawUint53`, `checkDigest`, `checkUUIDv7`, `checkTimestamp`, `checkSortedUniqueStrings`, `checkSortedUniqueDigests`, `checkStringBounds` duplicate `environ.CheckUint53Bounds/CheckDigest/CheckUUIDv7/CheckTimestamp/CheckSortedUniqueStrings/CheckSortedUniqueDigests/CheckStringBounds`, which sessadapter and dirnode already delegate to. Delegate, and keep only the package-specific rules (sanitizer, exclusion, coupling, reconciliation). That also fixes P2-a: `string[n..m]` bounds UTF-8 CHARACTERS (SPEC.v0.6.0.md:337) while `clonebundle.stringLength` returns `len(value)` in bytes and its comment asserts the opposite — rune-count every Build-side `len()` bound too, fix the comment, and add one multibyte boundary row (a 300-character / 600-byte value must be admitted where the bound is 512).\\n4. P2-b + P2-c — make the Decode-side gates measured. Every row in the reviewer's table (R-capture-items-dup-decode, R-sorted-digests-dup-decode, R-sorted-strings-dup-decode, R-rawrefs-dup-decode, R-decode-item-class, R-decode-actor-main-parent, R-decode-evidence-status, R-decode-event-visibility, R-verify-descriptors-skip-0, R-install-site-claim) survived the whole suite 3/3 because the rules are pinned only on the Build side and re-implemented on the Decode side where no named test reaches them; the arms themselves work (probes P10-P12 all refused), so this is missing EVIDENCE, not broken code. Add Decode-side negatives so every one of those rows is KILLED, add the missing negatives for `VerifyRawManifestDescriptors` (its three refusal arms) and for `InstallRawBlob`'s own claim comparison and blob_id/size arms, extend the shipped harness with those rows, and write ONE RAW LOG PER PLANT with the subprocess exit (P3-e — the current harness keeps verdict lines only). Replace `N-exclusion-config` (an arm-delete `&& false`) with a real narrowing (P3-d), and either split `N-descriptor-claim` per site or fix its note: it claims two measured sites while only the VerifyDescriptorAgreement site produces the kill.\\n5. P2-d — `deriveRawComplete` returns true for `rawKeys=[a,a]` against two included items because the \\\"all and only\\\" check compares lengths and then only tests membership, so a duplicate defeats the \\\"all\\\" half (planKeys duplicates ARE refused — the two halves are asymmetric). Refuse duplicate raw keys in `VerifyCaptureReconciliation`; negative + narrowing mutant. P2-e — `IdentityDigest`/`EncodeNativeIdentity` seal identities that `DecodeNativeIdentity` refuses (bad `identity_kind`, extension key without a dot), while the matrix names `IdentityDigest` as the `source_identity_digest` production entry and §13.14.1 defines that member as the digest of canonical SANITIZED bytes; re-decode before hashing exactly as `buildCaptureManifest` already does, and add the three negatives plus a narrowing mutant.\\n6. P3 — state or enforce each bound, do not leave it implicit: P3-a the sanitizer admits U+0085, U+2028, U+200B, `URN:AX:` in other case and schemeless `user:secret@host/path` — either harden (`unicode.IsControl`, case-fold the AX marker) or write the exact bound into TRACEABILITY.md; P3-b content-block members are a producer design absent from the pinned text and a block with neither content nor descriptor and one with both are both admitted — enforce \\\"typed inline content XOR Blob Descriptor reference\\\" or state the bound; P3-c `BuildCaptureManifest` seals items != plan candidates with `raw_complete=false` instead of refusing, while §13.14.1 says one item per plan candidate and §7.8 requires exact plan-key reconciliation — refuse at Build or justify the archive-only object; P3-g `BoundaryInput.Core` is a caller-asserted boolean so \\\"only core may construct\\\" is nominal at that entry — state that the real protection is `RefuseUnstableForTarget`; P3-f fix the LOGBOOK OWNERSHIP sentence (production calls neither `VerifyObjectIdentity` nor `Canonicalize` the way it claims; only the test fixture does) and stop claiming in results.md that it was already corrected; P3-h delete the dead code (`_ = extensionBytes`, `_ = manifest`, `_ = workspace`, `kindOf`) and the wrong-unit comment at decode.go:111.\\nProcedure: the managed Story worktree .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree (branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-1cyj0q) holds your uncommitted candidate on base 888ae3d, which is current origin/main — do not refresh unless origin/main actually moves, and if it does, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-24z2b3` and then audit every path in `git diff --name-only 888ae3d <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`, restoring trunk content by hand (README/LOGBOOK purely additive; task-board.config.json byte-identical to the new base). Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept. Then fix, re-run the full configured 27-command validation suite once (command 5 `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m` can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs — expected, do not shorten it), update TASK-260830-24z2b3_results.md with a finding-by-finding table (finding -> change -> the test that fails without it -> evidence path), update TASK-260830-24z2b3_conformance-matrix.md (rows 4, 6, 7, 12, 13, 15, 16, 17, 18 are the ones the reviewer found unmeasured or over-claimed) and TASK-260830-24z2b3_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive with one raw log per plant, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__), reset and re-complete the checklist, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-24z2b3 --role developer` so revision 3 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in; if something cannot be fixed as written, implement everything else and enumerate the remainder precisely with the reason. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The same claude-opus-5 max reviewer contract applies to revision 3.\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 16674,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 4169,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"bash","outcome":"success"}}}
